<p>A shop's product cache has worked well for months. <Term kind="redis">Redis</Term> answers in a fraction of a millisecond and the hit ratio is high. Then someone notices that, once a night, the slowest product-page requests, the 99.9th percentile, jump to about 100 ms for a moment. Redis's CPU graph barely moves, there is no error and no failover, and the spike is gone before anyone can look. The only thing that runs at that hour is a clean-up job that finds discontinued products with <code>KEYS product:*</code> and deletes their keys.</p>

<p>Up to now this collection has treated Redis as a fast box that answers <code>GET</code> and <code>SET</code>. That is enough to design a cache, but not to explain the nightly spike, or why a Redis holding 100 MB of data can occupy 200 MB of RAM, or why storing small values as fields of a hash rather than as separate keys can cut their memory by more than half. All three come from a few facts about how Redis is built: one thread runs every command, every command has a cost you can know in advance, and memory is allocated in ways that do not always give it back.</p>

<p>This article goes through those facts in order, measuring each on a real Redis 8.10 server. By the end you will be able to explain the nightly spike in full and fix it, predict which commands are dangerous on which keys, estimate what a key costs in memory, read the memory numbers Redis reports, and pick the right tool when latency jumps. The measurements come from my laptop, a Mac with the client on the same machine, so a production server on Linux across a real network will give different absolute numbers, and in two places, which I point out, different behaviour. Each figure says which of its numbers are measured and which come from a model.</p>

<h2 id="event-loop">One thread and an event loop</h2>

<p>A Redis server is one process, and in that process one thread, the <strong>main thread</strong>, executes every command. Thousands of clients can be connected at once, each with its own TCP connection. The main thread serves them with an <strong>event loop</strong>: it asks the operating system which connections have data waiting (through <code>epoll</code> on Linux, <code>kqueue</code> on macOS), reads and parses the requests on those connections, executes them one after another, writes the replies, and asks again.</p>

<p>Figure 1 runs that loop. Each client sends <code>GET</code>, waits for the reply and sends the next request. Under the drawing, a timeline shows what the main thread did over the last 0.4 ms. Grey blocks are work on sockets: reading a request, writing a reply. The thin coloured blocks are the thread <Term kind="redis">executing a command</Term>. Gaps are the thread waiting for something to do.</p>

<EventLoopFigure number={1} />

<p>The slider starts at one client, so the thread is idle most of the time. Notice that the client is not waiting for Redis; it is waiting for the network. Its request spends 50 µs travelling to the server, about 7.5 µs being served, and 50 µs travelling back, so it can make about 9,300 requests a second however fast Redis is.</p>

<p>Now drag the slider up, giving the readouts a few seconds to settle after each move. Each client adds about the same again, until somewhere around 14 clients the gaps disappear. From there, adding clients does not raise the requests a second at all. It only makes each request wait longer before the thread gets to it. One thread has a ceiling, and it is set by how long the thread spends on each request.</p>

<p>Look closely at a single request's work and notice how thin the coloured part is. Executing <code>GET</code> is a hash-table lookup; the expensive part is the socket work around it. I measured this. I ran <code>redis-benchmark</code> against the server with 100 connections sending <code>GET</code>, first one request at a time and then with 16 requests sent together in each round trip (that is <strong>pipelining</strong>, the subject of <ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining">the next article</ArticleRef>), and compared the CPU time the server used:</p>

<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Server CPU per GET with and without pipelining">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Requests per round trip</th><th scope="col">Requests a second</th><th scope="col">Server CPU per request</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>128,000 to 130,000</td><td>7.55 µs</td></tr>
    <tr><td>16</td><td>1,430,000</td><td>0.68 µs</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>Call the cost of one round trip's socket work <em>s</em> and the cost of one command <em>e</em>. Without pipelining every request pays both; with 16 to a round trip, the socket cost is shared sixteen ways:</p>

<p class="redis-formula">s + e = 7.55 µs<br />s ÷ 16 + e = 0.68 µs<br />so s × 15⁄16 = 6.87 µs, s ≈ 7.3 µs and e ≈ 0.2 µs</p>

<p>About 97% of the thread's time per request goes on the work of each round trip (the system calls that read the request and write the reply, and the event loop's own bookkeeping) and 3% on the command, which here includes parsing the request and building the reply. Redis's own accounting puts the command lower still: <code>INFO commandstats</code> reported 0.05 µs per <code>GET</code>, because it times only the command's own function. These are costs of macOS's <code>kqueue</code> and loopback networking; on Linux the split will differ, and I have not measured it there. The ceiling follows from the same number. One thread that spends 7.55 µs per request can serve at most 1 ÷ 7.55 µs, about 132,000 requests a second, which is what the benchmark reached. The thread was at about 100% of one CPU core while the machine had 13 more.</p>

<p>The single thread is a deliberate choice, and it buys something valuable. Because only one command ever runs at a time, every command is <strong>atomic</strong>: no other client can see it half done. <code>INCR</code> on a counter never loses an update, and <code>SET</code> with <code>NX</code> can act as a lock, with no locking inside Redis at all. The cost is the other side of the same fact: while one command runs, nothing else does.</p>

<h2 id="slow-command">What one slow command does to everyone</h2>

<p>Most commands take a microsecond or less, so taking turns is invisible. The clean-up job's <code>KEYS</code> is not like most commands. <code>KEYS pattern</code> returns every key name that matches a pattern, and to find them it looks at every key in the database.</p>

<p>I loaded a million keys into the server and ran <code>KEYS *</code> while a second client sent <code>PING</code> about once a millisecond and timed each reply. <code>KEYS</code> held the main thread for 99.4 ms, and the slowest <code>PING</code> took 100.1 ms instead of the usual 0.1 ms. Figure 2 replays that at a pace you can follow, slowed down 50 times.</p>

<MainThreadFigure number={2} mode="keys" />

<p>Before you press the button, watch the timeline: thin slivers of work, a few hundred requests a second, and on the chart one stem per <code>PING</code>, each about 0.1 ms tall, too short to see. Now run <code>KEYS *</code>. One block covers the timeline. The <Term kind="client" mark>PING waiting</Term> in the lane below is the one that arrived while <code>KEYS</code> ran: it waits for all of it, and so does every other request; by the time the block ends about 40 of them are queued. When the block ends, the queue drains in well under a millisecond and nothing on the chart suggests anything was wrong, apart from one stem.</p>

<p>Now set the speed to 4× and try 10 million keys. The block lasts a second, and its stem stands ten times taller than the first one, which is still on the chart beside it. This is called <strong>head-of-line blocking</strong>: the request at the head of the line holds up everyone behind it, however cheap their own requests are.</p>

<p>That is the nightly spike. A <code>KEYS product:*</code> over a million keys does the same thing: on a single server, the pattern does not let Redis skip anything. On my server <code>KEYS user:0000099*</code>, which matched only 10 keys, still held the thread for 78 ms, and <code>KEYS nomatch:*</code>, which matched none, for 50 ms. And it explains the flat CPU graph: 100 ms of one core, once a night, is invisible on a graph that averages over a minute.</p>

<h2 id="complexity">Knowing in advance which commands are slow</h2>

<p>You do not have to find slow commands by accident. Every command's page in the Redis documentation states its <strong>time complexity</strong>: how its work grows with the size of its input. <code>KEYS</code> is O(N) where N is the number of keys in the database. <code>GET</code> and <code>SET</code> are O(1): the same work whether the database holds a hundred keys or a hundred million.</p>

<p>The part to read carefully is what N is. For some commands it is the whole keyspace; for many it is the number of elements in one key. A set, a hash, a list or a sorted set can hold millions of elements, and then an O(N) command on that one key is as slow as <code>KEYS</code>. Such a key is usually called a <strong>big key</strong>.</p>

<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Common commands and what their cost grows with">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Command</th><th scope="col">Cost grows with</th><th scope="col">Safe on a big key?</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>GET</code>, <code>SET</code>, <code>HGET</code>, <code>SADD</code>, <code>INCR</code></td><td>nothing (O(1))</td><td>yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ZADD</code>, <code>ZRANK</code></td><td>log of the set's size</td><td>yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>LRANGE</code>, <code>ZRANGE</code> with a small range</td><td>the elements returned (plus the offset, for lists)</td><td>yes, if the range is small</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>HGETALL</code>, <code>SMEMBERS</code>, <code>LRANGE 0 -1</code></td><td>every element of the key</td><td>no</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>DEL</code> of a set, hash, list or sorted set</td><td>every element of the key</td><td>no (see below)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>KEYS</code>, <code>FLUSHALL</code> without <code>ASYNC</code></td><td>every key in the database</td><td>never in production</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>There are two costs to an O(N) read on a big key, and only one of them is on the server. <code>SMEMBERS</code> of a 300,000-member set held the main thread for 13 ms on my server, and another client's <code>PING</code> waited 13 ms. But the client that asked took 90 ms to receive and parse the 300,000-element reply with my simple Python client. The server's thread is free once it has built the reply, while the network and the client are not.</p>

<p>A Lua script or a <code>MULTI</code> transaction is no different: it runs as one unit on the main thread, so a long one blocks everyone just as <code>KEYS</code> does. Once a script has run for longer than <code>busy-reply-threshold</code> (5 seconds by default), Redis starts answering other clients with a <code>BUSY</code> error instead of making them wait; <ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining">the next article</ArticleRef> covers scripts and transactions.</p>

<p>Two practical defences follow. First, find big keys before they cause a stall: <code>redis-cli --bigkeys</code> walks the keyspace and reports the largest key of each type (on my server it found the 300,000-member set among a million strings), and <code>--memkeys</code> does the same by memory. Second, keep <code>KEYS</code> out of production entirely. <code>KEYS</code>, <code>FLUSHALL</code>, <code>SORT</code> and <code>DEBUG</code> are all in Redis's <code>@dangerous</code> access-control category, so a user created with <code>-@dangerous</code> cannot run them at all.</p>

<p>But the clean-up job still needs to find the discontinued products' keys. What should it use instead?</p>

<h2 id="scan">SCAN: walking the keyspace in pieces</h2>

<p><code>SCAN</code> does the same walk as <code>KEYS</code>, but a piece at a time. Each call returns a few keys and a <strong>cursor</strong>, a number that says where to carry on; you pass the cursor to the next call, and the walk is finished when the cursor comes back as 0. Between calls the main thread is free to serve everyone else.</p>

<pre><code>{`cursor = 0
while True:
    cursor, keys = redis.scan(cursor, match="product:*", count=1000)
    handle(keys)
    if cursor == 0:
        break`}</code></pre>

<p>The <code>COUNT</code> option is a hint for how much of the keyspace each call should look at; it defaults to 10. Figure 3 shows what it changes. I walked the same million keys with <code>KEYS</code> and with <code>SCAN</code> at five values of <code>COUNT</code>, while a separate process sent <code>PING</code> about once a millisecond, and the figure replays each walk in real time. The bar is the job's progress, the strip is the main thread executing the walk, drawn to scale, and the chart is how long each <code>PING</code> took.</p>

<ScanFigure number={3} />

<p>Start with <code>KEYS</code>. The job has every key after 0.11 s, quicker than any <code>SCAN</code>, but the chart shows one stem of 86 ms: everyone else waited that long. Now choose <code>COUNT</code> 10, the default, and watch the bar crawl. Each call took about 2 µs, almost too thin to see on the strip, with a network round trip between one call and the next, and the walk needed 95,420 calls and almost 10 seconds. Nothing on the chart comes from <code>SCAN</code>: its longest call was 0.07 ms, and the spikes are my laptop's own hiccups, which you can see before the walk begins as well.</p>

<p>Then step through the larger values. <code>COUNT</code> 1,000 finished in 0.33 s, and no call held the thread for more than 0.44 ms. <code>COUNT</code> 10,000 halves the walk to 0.17 s, and beyond that it gets no faster while the pauses grow: with <code>COUNT</code> 100,000 each call took about 16 ms, and the chart shows ten stems of that height. That is <code>KEYS</code> cut into ten pieces.</p>

<p>Notice that the total time on the main thread hardly changes, between 0.14 s and 0.25 s for every <code>SCAN</code>. <code>COUNT</code> does not make the walk cheaper. It decides how the work is cut up, and so trades how soon the walk finishes against how long any other client might wait. A <code>COUNT</code> in the hundreds or low thousands is a reasonable default for a job that walks a large keyspace.</p>

<p><code>SCAN</code> gives weaker guarantees than <code>KEYS</code>, because the keyspace can change while the walk is in progress. Its documentation promises that a key present for the whole walk is returned at least once. It may be returned more than once, so whatever you do with the keys must be safe to repeat. A key added or removed during the walk may or may not appear. And <code>MATCH</code> is applied after the keys are fetched, so a call can return no keys at all and still not be the end: on my server, <code>SCAN 0 MATCH user:0000099* COUNT 10</code> returned an empty page and cursor 65536. Only a cursor of 0 means the walk is done.</p>

<p>For the clean-up job, <code>SCAN</code> with <code>MATCH product:*</code> and a <code>COUNT</code> of 1,000 removes the nightly 100 ms pause. Better still, a job that keeps a set of the keys it will need to clean up, or gives them a TTL (time to live) when it writes them, as <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> describes, never has to walk the keyspace at all. If any of those keys is a big collection, the job's deletes can stall Redis too.</p>

<h2 id="freeing">Deleting a big key: DEL, UNLINK and lazy freeing</h2>

<p>Deleting a key is not just removing its name. Redis has to give every one of the key's allocations back to the memory allocator, and a big collection is made of millions of small allocations. On my server, <code>DEL</code> of a set with 2,000,000 members held the main thread for 195 ms.</p>

<p><code>UNLINK</code> exists for this. It removes the key from the keyspace at once, so no client can see it any more, and hands the freeing to a background thread. Figure 4 shows both, with the same set and the same probing client. The second lane is the background thread, <Term kind="redis">freeing memory</Term>.</p>

<MainThreadFigure number={4} mode="free" />

<p>Run <code>DEL</code> first, and the 195 ms block lands on the main thread with the <code>PING</code> stuck behind it, just like <code>KEYS</code>. Then switch to <code>UNLINK</code>. The same 195 ms of work appears on the other lane, and the <code>PING</code>s never notice. On the real server, <code>UNLINK</code> replied in 0.2 ms and the slowest <code>PING</code> during it was 0.3 ms.</p>

<p>Redis only hands the work to the background thread when it is worth it. In the 8.10 source, a value is freed in the background when freeing it would take more than 64 allocations. A string is a single allocation however long it is, so I measured <code>DEL</code> of a 100 MB string at 0.2 ms; freeing one large block is quick. The danger is collections with many elements.</p>

<p>Now try the third option: give the key a TTL and let it run out. The block is back on the main thread. When Redis deletes a key by itself, because it expired or because it was evicted to stay under <code>maxmemory</code>, it frees the value on the main thread by default. That happens whether the expiry cycle finds the key or a command touches it first: on my server an <code>EXISTS</code> on the expired set returned about 215 ms late. Several settings move each kind of deletion to the background thread:</p>

<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Lazy-free settings and their defaults">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">Applies to</th><th scope="col">Default in Redis 8.10</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>lazyfree-lazy-expire</code></td><td>keys deleted because their TTL ran out</td><td><code>no</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>lazyfree-lazy-eviction</code></td><td>keys evicted under <code>maxmemory</code></td><td><code>no</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>lazyfree-lazy-server-del</code></td><td>keys a command overwrites or deletes as a side effect, such as <code>SET</code> on an existing set</td><td><code>no</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>lazyfree-lazy-user-del</code></td><td>makes <code>DEL</code> behave like <code>UNLINK</code></td><td><code>no</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>lazyfree-lazy-user-flush</code></td><td><code>FLUSHALL</code> and <code>FLUSHDB</code> without <code>SYNC</code> or <code>ASYNC</code></td><td><code>no</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>replica-lazy-flush</code></td><td>a replica's old data, discarded when it copies the whole dataset from its primary again</td><td><code>no</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>I measured the expiry case both ways. With the default, the set expiring took the slowest <code>PING</code> to 194 ms and 211 ms in two runs. With <code>lazyfree-lazy-expire yes</code>, the fourth option in Figure 4, the free moves to the background thread just as with <code>UNLINK</code>. On the real server the slowest <code>PING</code> was then 7 to 24 ms in three runs. Over repeated runs, the slowest <code>PING</code> was between 0.4 and 14 ms for <code>UNLINK</code> and for lazy expiry alike, and <code>PING</code>s before either command reached similar values, so those milliseconds belong to my busy laptop, and perhaps to the two threads sharing the allocator, rather than to expiry. With <code>lazyfree-lazy-user-del yes</code>, a plain <code>DEL</code> of the big set replied in 0.2 ms.</p>

<p>For a cache, where big values come and go through expiry and eviction, it is reasonable to turn all of these on. Valkey, a fork of Redis, sets all six to <code>yes</code> by default in version 8.0, including <code>lazyfree-lazy-user-del</code>, so there a plain <code>DEL</code> already behaves like <code>UNLINK</code>. Check which defaults your server has rather than assuming. And in your own code, use <code>UNLINK</code> rather than <code>DEL</code> for anything that might be a big collection; it costs nothing on small keys.</p>

<h2 id="io-threads">I/O threads</h2>

<p>If the main thread spends 97% of its time per request on socket work, the obvious improvement is to move the socket work to other threads. Redis 6.0 added <strong>I/O threads</strong> for that, and Redis 8.0 replaced the implementation. With <code>io-threads</code> set above 1, the main thread assigns each client connection to an I/O thread. The I/O thread reads and parses the client's requests, the main thread executes them, and the I/O thread writes the replies. Figure 5 follows one request through.</p>

<IoThreadsFigure number={5} />

<p>Step through it and notice what stays on the main thread: executing the command. That is what keeps every command atomic, and it is also why I/O threads do nothing for the nightly spike. A <code>KEYS</code> that takes 100 ms still takes 100 ms on the main thread, and every client still waits for it. Some connections never move to an I/O thread at all: those in Pub/Sub mode, those running <code>MONITOR</code>, those waiting in a blocking command such as <code>BLPOP</code>, and those using client-side caching (<code>CLIENT TRACKING</code>) stay on the main thread.</p>

<p>What I/O threads change is the ceiling from the first section. Redis's own figures claim gains of 37% to 112% in throughput for the 8.0 implementation with 8 threads, and more than 30% for a caching workload on 4 cores in the 8.4 release notes; those are the vendor's benchmarks. I tried <code>io-threads 4</code> against 1 on my laptop and could not separate the result from noise, with the benchmark client competing for the same cores, so I have no measurement of my own to add.</p>

<p>The setting is off by default (<code>io-threads 1</code>) and can only be set at start-up. <code>io-threads N</code> runs the main thread plus N − 1 I/O threads. The configuration file advises enabling it only on a machine with at least 4 cores, leaving at least one core spare, and only once the server is using a large share of its CPU. Its example (“if you have a four cores boxes, try to use 3 I/O threads”) was written before the 8.0 rewrite and can be read either way. The spare core is for the kernel's network processing, the lazy-free thread and any fork for persistence, so set <code>io-threads</code> to one less than the number of cores: <code>io-threads 3</code> on 4 cores, as in Figure 5, and 7 on 8. For a cache that is far from its ceiling, which is most of them, the network round trip matters much more, and <ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining">the next article</ArticleRef> is about that.</p>

<h2 id="key-memory">What one key costs in memory</h2>

<p>Redis keeps everything in memory, so the second question about any cache is how much memory it needs. The obvious estimate, the number of keys times the size of each value, is wrong for small values, because every key carries overhead that has nothing to do with its value.</p>

<p>To see how much, I wrote 200,000 keys with 13-byte names like <code>user:00000001</code> and string values of one size, measured how much Redis's memory grew, divided by 200,000, and repeated for sizes from 1 byte to 4,000. Figure 6 shows the result. The slider runs through the value sizes I measured.</p>

<KeyCostFigure number={6} />

<p>Start at the smallest value. On my Mac a 1-byte value cost 69 bytes per key, almost all of it overhead: an object header with the key's type, encoding and access information, the key's entry in the keyspace's hash table, and the allocator rounding each allocation up to a size it keeps. At 100 bytes, a typical small cached value, a key cost 159 bytes, so more than a third of the memory was not the value. Only at a few kilobytes does the value dominate.</p>

<p>Now switch on the TTL. Each key with an expiry stores its 8-byte expiry time in its own object, and also has an entry in a second hash table, the table of keys that have one. Together that cost between 21 and 42 bytes per key. At 10 million keys of 100 bytes that is the difference between 1.6 GB and 2.0 GB.</p>

<p>Watch the readout for <code>MEMORY USAGE</code>, the command that reports one key's memory. It reported 144 bytes for a key that cost 159, and only 8 bytes more, the stored expiry time, for a key with a TTL that cost 42 bytes more. It counts the key's own allocations, not its share of the keyspace's tables, so it is the right tool for comparing keys and an underestimate for planning capacity. For capacity, measure what <code>used_memory</code> in <code>INFO memory</code> grows by when you load a sample of real keys, as I did.</p>

<p>One more detail shows in the figure, and it is one place where Linux and my Mac differ. A short string is stored in a single allocation with the object header and the key name, an encoding Redis calls <code>embstr</code>, as long as all of it fits in one CPU cache line. Redis 8 takes the cache line to be 64 bytes on every build except Apple Silicon, including any Linux server, so the key's length plus the value's must be at most 41 bytes: with 13-byte key names, values of up to 28 bytes. A longer value gets an allocation of its own (<code>raw</code>), which adds another allocation and its rounding. Builds for Apple Silicon use a 128-byte line, and a separate 44-byte limit then applies first, which is why the cost in Figure 6 steps up between 44 and 45 bytes; on a Linux server, with these keys, the step would be between 28 and 29. Integers that fit in 64 bits are stored as numbers (<code>int</code>). <code>OBJECT ENCODING key</code> tells you which one a key uses.</p>

<h2 id="encodings">Small hashes are packed: listpacks</h2>

<p>The overhead per key suggests a way to save memory: store many small values inside one key. A hash holds field–value pairs under one key name, so a thousand small values can share one key's overhead. Whether that pays off depends on how Redis stores the hash, and by default Redis has two ways.</p>

<p>A small hash is stored as a <strong>listpack</strong>: every field and value packed one after another in a single allocation, with no hash table and no pointers. Finding a field means reading through the pack from the start, which is fast when the pack is small. A large hash is stored as a <strong>hash table</strong>, like the keyspace itself: an array of buckets, with a separate allocation for each field and value. Redis switches from the first to the second when the hash grows past either of two limits: more than 512 fields (<code>hash-max-listpack-entries</code>), or any field name or value longer than 64 bytes (<code>hash-max-listpack-value</code>).</p>

<p>Figure 7 grows one hash a field at a time. The chart is measured: <code>MEMORY USAGE</code> divided by the number of fields, after each <code>HSET</code>.</p>

<EncodingFigure number={7} />

<p>With 20-byte values, the hash cost about 34 bytes per field while it was a listpack, barely more than the 30 bytes of field name and value. The dashed line is the same values stored as separate keys with names of the same length: 85 bytes each. As fields of a packed hash they cost 60% less. That is the saving the opening promised, and it lasts only while the hash stays a listpack. Drag the slider past 512 and watch the 513th field arrive: the hash converts to a hash table, and its memory doubles, from 17,437 bytes to 34,946. Now switch to 100-byte values. Every value is over the 64-byte limit, so the hash is a hash table from its first field, at 140 to 160 bytes per field, close to the 165 bytes of a separate key.</p>

<p>Two more facts matter in practice. For hashes, sets and sorted sets the conversion goes one way only: deleting fields from a hash table never turns it back into a listpack. (Lists do shrink back.) And the same scheme applies to other types, with their own limits:</p>

<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Compact encodings and their default limits">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Compact encoding</th><th scope="col">Becomes</th><th scope="col">Default limits (Redis 8.10)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Hash</td><td>listpack</td><td>hashtable</td><td>512 fields, values up to 64 bytes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Set of integers</td><td>intset</td><td>hashtable (or listpack)</td><td>512 members</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Set of strings</td><td>listpack</td><td>hashtable</td><td>128 members, members up to 64 bytes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sorted set</td><td>listpack</td><td>skiplist</td><td>128 members, members up to 64 bytes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>List</td><td>listpack</td><td>quicklist (a list of listpacks)</td><td>about 8 KB per pack</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>Redis 8.10 added a third way to store a hash, the compact hash (encoding <code>template-listpack</code>): hashes with the same set of field names share one copy of the names instead of each repeating them. It is off by default (<code>hash-min-template-entries 0</code>). Set a minimum field count and hashes with at least that many fields convert as they are written; the conversion is one-way. On my server, 200,000 hashes with the same five field names and 20-byte values took 191 bytes each instead of 261, about a quarter less.</p>

<p>Field expiry, which lets single fields of a hash carry their own TTL through <code>HEXPIRE</code>, interacts with these encodings. The first <code>HEXPIRE</code> on a small hash converts it to a fourth form, <code>listpackex</code>: a listpack that keeps the fields in order of expiry time and stores an expiry entry, two bytes when no TTL is set, after every value. On my server a three-field hash grew from 42 to 80 bytes of <code>MEMORY USAGE</code> when one field was given a TTL. A compact hash that is given a field expiry leaves the compact form and becomes a <code>listpackex</code> (a hash table if it is over the listpack limits), and it is never made compact again, so if you use field expiry, the savings above do not apply to those hashes.</p>

<p>I checked each threshold on the server with <code>OBJECT ENCODING</code>: an integer set converted at its 513th member, a string set at its 129th, a sorted set at its 129th. You can raise the limits, and some guides suggest it to save memory, but every access to a listpack reads through it, so a pack of thousands of entries trades memory for CPU on the main thread. Whether to store a group of cached values as fields of one hash or as separate keys is a design decision with other consequences, such as expiry: <ArticleRef slug="keys-and-values">Cache keys and cached values</ArticleRef> covers it, and these are the numbers to bring to it.</p>

<h2 id="fragmentation">used_memory, RSS and fragmentation</h2>

<p>Redis reports its memory in two ways that are easy to confuse. <code>used_memory</code> is what the allocator has handed Redis for its live allocations, including the allocator's rounding. <code>used_memory_rss</code> is the resident set size (RSS), what the operating system has actually given the process, in pages. Their ratio is <code>mem_fragmentation_ratio</code>. <code>maxmemory</code> and eviction work on <code>used_memory</code>, but it is RSS that runs the machine out of RAM.</p>

<p>Normally the two are close. I loaded a million 100-byte values into a fresh server and then deleted every second key:</p>

<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="used_memory and RSS while loading and deleting keys">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">used_memory</th><th scope="col">RSS</th><th scope="col">Ratio</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>1,000,000 keys loaded</td><td>159 MB</td><td>171 MB</td><td>1.07</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Every second key deleted</td><td>84 MB</td><td>171 MB</td><td>2.03</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500,000 new keys added</td><td>168 MB</td><td>196 MB</td><td>1.17</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Everything deleted (<code>FLUSHALL</code>)</td><td>1 MB</td><td>192 MB</td><td>189</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>Deleting half the data did not give back a single megabyte. Figure 8 shows why, with a simplified allocator. Memory comes from the operating system in pages, and each page holds many allocations. A page can be returned only when every allocation on it is free.</p>

<PagesFigure number={8} />

<p>With half the keys deleted at random, every page still holds about half its keys. For a page of 16 slots to be completely empty, all 16 of its keys must have been among the deleted ones, and the chance of that is 0.5<sup>16</sup>, about one in 65,000. So no page goes back, used memory halves, resident memory stays where it was, and the ratio is 2, as on the real server. Now drag the second slider. New keys fill the holes first, so the ratio falls back towards 1 without RSS growing. On the real server, adding 500,000 keys back mostly reused the holes: <code>used_memory</code> doubled again while RSS grew by 15%.</p>

<p>The model is the best case in one respect: it hands an empty page back at once. A real allocator decides when to return empty pages, and the last row of the table shows that it may not. After <code>FLUSHALL</code>, with almost nothing left, my laptop's allocator still held 192 MB. An empty page can be returned; whether it is depends on the allocator.</p>

<p>Three practical consequences follow. First, provision memory for the peak, not the current size: the Redis documentation says plainly that RSS reflects the peak, and that is the memory the machine must have. Because <code>maxmemory</code> counts <code>used_memory</code>, set it well below the machine's RAM, with room for RSS to run ahead of it; after a mass delete it ran to twice <code>used_memory</code> here. <ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache">Operating a cache</ArticleRef> covers sizing. Second, a high <code>mem_fragmentation_ratio</code> after a large deletion is not a problem in itself; the space will be reused, and the ratio is unreliable until it is. A ratio well below 1, on the other hand, usually means part of Redis's memory is in swap, which is a serious latency problem.</p>

<p>Third, do not read the table above as typical of production. My laptop's Redis uses the operating system's allocator, which kept freed pages and ignored <code>MEMORY PURGE</code>, the command that asks the allocator to hand unused pages back. Redis on Linux is normally built with the jemalloc allocator. It rounds allocations to different sizes, returns empty pages gradually in the background, and reports its own fragmentation as <code>allocator_frag_ratio</code> in <code>INFO memory</code>.</p>

<p>jemalloc also makes <strong>active defragmentation</strong> possible (<code>activedefrag yes</code>, off by default). Redis moves values out of sparsely used pages so that whole pages can be returned. The work runs on the main thread, between 1% and 25% of its CPU by default, so it is worth turning on when <code>allocator_frag_ratio</code> stays high and memory is short, not after every mass delete that new writes will soon fill in.</p>

<h2 id="diagnosis">Finding the cause of a latency spike</h2>

<p>Back to the nightly spike. Suppose you did not know about the clean-up job. Which tool would have told you? Redis has several, and each sees a different part of a stall. Figure 9 shows one <code>KEYS *</code> holding the main thread for about 100 ms, a <code>GET</code> from the application stuck behind it, and <code>redis-cli --latency</code> running alongside. Choose a tool to see what it reports; the outputs are copied from my runs.</p>

<ToolsFigure number={9} />

<p>The important distinction is between the culprit and the victims. The <strong>slow log</strong> (<code>SLOWLOG GET</code>) records every command whose execution took longer than <code>slowlog-log-slower-than</code>, 10,000 µs by default, keeping the last 128 entries. It names the culprit, the time and the client that sent it. It never lists the victims, because it times execution only, and the victims executed in microseconds. Their time was spent waiting.</p>

<p>The victims show up in timers outside the main thread: your application's own latency metrics, and <code>redis-cli --latency</code>, which sends <code>PING</code> from outside as any client would, waits for the reply, sleeps 10 ms and repeats, printing the minimum, maximum and average round trip. In my three runs during a <code>KEYS *</code> of 91 to 98 ms, its maximum was 89 to 98 ms while its average stayed near 0.5 ms. Read the maximum; the average hides the stall. In a script, where its output is not a terminal, it prints one line after a second and exits, so give it <code>-i</code> with the number of seconds to sample, or use <code>--latency-history</code>.</p>

<p>Between those sit the <strong>latency monitor</strong> and the per-command statistics. The latency monitor records events that exceed <code>latency-monitor-threshold</code>, and it is off until you set one (the default is 0). It records non-command events too, such as <code>fork</code> and <code>expire-cycle</code>, which makes it the tool for stalls that are not caused by any command. <code>INFO latencystats</code> and <code>INFO commandstats</code> accumulate execution time per command type, so a command that is slow on average stands out there even when the slow log has rotated.</p>

<p>Not every stall is a command. Saving a snapshot or rewriting the append-only file starts with a <code>fork</code> of the whole process, which pauses the main thread for roughly 10 ms per gigabyte of memory on modern hardware, so a few hundred milliseconds for a cache of tens of gigabytes, and far longer on some older virtual machines. On Linux, transparent huge pages make the latency after a fork worse, and the Redis documentation recommends turning them off. <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence">Redis replication, persistence and failover</ArticleRef> covers both. A server that has started swapping is slow everywhere. Very many keys expiring in the same moment keep the expiry cycle busy, which <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> covers. And sometimes the host itself is the problem: <code>redis-cli --intrinsic-latency 5</code>, run on the server's own host rather than the client's, measures how long the machine takes to schedule a process that does nothing else. On my laptop the average run took 1 µs and the worst 1,152 times that, about 1.2 ms, a floor no Redis setting can go below.</p>

<h2 id="decisions">What you can now decide</h2>

<p>The nightly spike now has a full explanation. <code>KEYS product:*</code> examined every key in the database on the one thread that serves every client, about 100 ms for a million keys; every request that arrived in that time waited for it; the slow log recorded it, and nothing else did unless someone was timing from outside. The fix is to walk with <code>SCAN</code> and a <code>COUNT</code> of a few hundred to a few thousand, delete with <code>UNLINK</code>, and, better, stop needing the walk by tracking or expiring the keys the job cares about.</p>

<p>More generally, you can now:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Predict which commands are dangerous on which keys from their stated time complexity and what N is, and find big keys with <code>redis-cli --bigkeys</code> before they cause trouble.</li>
  <li>Walk a keyspace or a big collection with <code>SCAN</code> (or <code>HSCAN</code>, <code>SSCAN</code>, <code>ZSCAN</code>), choosing <code>COUNT</code> as a trade between how long the walk takes and how long anyone else waits.</li>
  <li>Delete big values without a stall, with <code>UNLINK</code> and the <code>lazyfree-*</code> settings, and know that Redis and Valkey differ in their defaults.</li>
  <li>Decide whether I/O threads could help: only when the main thread is busy with socket work, and never for a slow command; if they can, set <code>io-threads</code> to one less than the number of cores.</li>
  <li>Estimate memory from measured bytes per key rather than value sizes (a TTL added 21 to 42 bytes on my server), and use the listpack limits when choosing between hashes and separate keys.</li>
  <li>Read <code>used_memory</code> against RSS, provision for the peak with <code>maxmemory</code> well below the machine's RAM, and recognise when a high fragmentation ratio is harmless.</li>
  <li>Choose the tool for a latency spike: the slow log for the culprit, client-side timers and <code>--latency</code> for the victims, the latency monitor for stalls that are not commands.</li>
</ul>

<p>One number from the first section is worth carrying forward: on my server, 97% of the main thread's time per request went on the work of each round trip, not on the command. On top of that work, the application waits for the network on every round trip. <ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining">Redis clients: round trips, pipelining and atomic operations</ArticleRef> is about making fewer round trips.</p>

